{
  "id": "binaural-masking-level-difference",
  "term": "binaural masking level difference",
  "definition": "Binaural masking level difference is a lab measure of the binaural hearing advantage for finding a sound in background noise. It means the change in the faintest detectable signal level when the relationship between the sound and the masker differs across the two ears, often by comparing a same-phase condition with one in which the signal is reversed in phase at one ear.",
